#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cono-check — Cono's checking component as a local web app: a live
digital twin of the page on your desk.

The camera watches your page; the page diffs the video locally, and only
when you pause writing does one frame go to Claude (`claude` CLI — your
Claude Code login, no API key), which streams back, fast fields first:

  visibility n/10   what the camera can actually see
  problem card      the question you're on, your progress, a Next button
  check             hint-first: where the mistake is, never the answer
  readability map   green/red page-shaped grid: what could/couldn't be read
  equations         the relations the problem runs on, ? for unknowns
  on your page      faithful transcription, student's errors preserved

The twin is the product: problem card + dialogue, equations, the
transcribed page, readability map. The twin is also the pointing
surface: click a transcribed line, a relation, the problem card, or a
readability cell to anchor a typed question to that exact thing. The
Next button advances the problem card and pins it there until your
writing genuinely moves on. Questions, once read clearly, live in a
registry and never reword themselves because a hand covered the sheet. Chem/math is typeset (H2O-style subscripts,
charges, arrows) everywhere it appears. Replies stream — first text in
~1-3 s.

Usage:
  cono-check                     # the Desk View camera (top-down on your desk) - the default
  cono-check browser             # fallback: any browser camera (iPhone Continuity, webcam)
  cono-check demo                # no camera needed: bundled sample session (demo chem = chemistry)
  cono-check doctor              # preflight: is everything ready to run?
  cono-check --model sonnet      # pick the model (default: opus)
  cono-check --replay DIR        # replay any recorded session directory
  cono-check --port 9000         # serve somewhere else (default: 8764)
  cono-check --contrast strong   # writing clarity: off | auto (default) | strong
  cono-check --no-contrast       # same as --contrast off
  cono-check --no-record         # testdev opt-out: run live without recording
  cono-check --dev               # start in dev mode (show instrumentation)
  cono-check --view ambient      # start in the ambient face (default: twin)
  cono-check --no-open           # don't open the browser automatically
Ctrl-C to stop.

STUDY vs DEV. The page opens in study mode: just the twin — problem card,
relations, page, map, dialogue, cone — with automatic defaults. The quiet
"dev" switch (or --dev) reveals the instrumentation together: stage
trace, writing-clarity cycler, per-check latency and clarity tags, the
bundle path. Modes change only what is SHOWN; recording and per-check
metadata never stop. An always-reachable "Suggest an improvement" box
(both modes) appends each suggestion, timestamped with its context
(problem, last check, frame, mode/config), to the session bundle and to
~/deskcap/suggestions.jsonl, which accumulates across sessions.

TESTDEV MODE - every live run records. From launch until "End & rate"
(or the server stops), each live run (deskview or browser) saves a full
session bundle locally: a dense photo of the page (every 3 s for the
first 10 minutes, every 10 s after), every snapshot read, every note,
chat, pen-point and button press - all in ~/deskcap/session-<timestamp>/.
Ending shows the rating pass (Helped / Didn't help / Wrong per note -
skippable); the bundle is complete and scoreable either way, and one
click deletes it. The server prints the folder size every 10 minutes and
at the end. Nothing leaves the machine beyond the snaps Claude already
reads. Opt out with --no-record. (`session` is accepted as a no-op alias
from the 10-minute era.) Demo/replay runs never record.

Snaps get a writing-clarity pass before the model reads them. "auto"
(default) is a global histogram stretch - near-no-op on well-lit pages, a
lift on faint pencil. "strong" goes after faint strokes: flatten uneven
lighting (subtract a blurred background), stretch the flattened contrast,
gamma mid-gray graphite toward black, lightly sharpen stroke edges
(grayscale output). Browser snaps are processed on the canvas before
upload; deskview/replay frames in place server-side when Pillow is
installed (optional - everything works without it). The stored snap, the
thumbnail, and what the model reads are always the same pixels; each
check logs which pass its snap got. Switch mid-session with the "writing
clarity" button (dev mode), or start with --contrast MODE.

Test feature: a "check stages" strip in the side column traces each
in-flight check/chat through the real pipeline stages (contrast, sent,
first streamed word, then each reply section as it arrives) with elapsed
time - driven by server events, never a timer. Toggle it with the
"stage trace" button; per-stage ms is logged into session bundles and
tabled by tools/score-session.py.
"""
